{"classified_as":[{"_label":"Collection Item","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404024","type":"Type"}],"id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/touring-exhibition/886","identified_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"preferred term","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670"}],"content":"New Mexico and New York: Photographs of Georgia O'Keeffe","type":"Name"}],"produced_by":{"carried_out_by":[{"_label":"Georgia O'Keeffe Museum","id":"actor/1","type":"Actor"}],"timespan":{"type":"TimeSpan"},"type":"Production"},"referred_to_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"description statement","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"}],"content":"This exhibition features for the first time approximately thirty works dating from 1917 through the 1960s from the Museum’s expanding photography collection.  These images of Georgia O’Keeffe were made in the two areas of the country that have been most closely associated with her achievement: New York and New Mexico. With the exception of several excursions to Maine and Wisconsin in the 1920s, O’Keeffe lived year-round in New York with her husband, Alfred Stieglitz, from 1918 to 1929, when she began spending part of the year working in New Mexico.  Stieglitz first photographed O’Keeffe in 1917, when she visited New York briefly to see the exhibition of her work that Stieglitz had organized at his famous gallery, 291.  From then until his retirement from photography in 1937, Stieglitz continued to photograph O’Keeffe either in New York, where the two spent winter and spring, or at the Stieglitz family compound at Lake George, where they spent summer and fall. The exhibition includes examples of Stieglitz photographs of O’Keeffe dating from 1917 through 1935, that were either made in the city and at Lake George, and it includes examples of other Stieglitz photographs of Lake George subjects.","type":"LinguisticObject"}],"representation":[],"type":"Activity"}